> > > > > > Hi, thank you for your message. It is wonderful to see scholars in other countries are discovering the open-source tool that we are developing to carry out latent profile analysis. At this present time, you can only carry out the parametric bootstrapped BLRT with the boostrap_lrt() function.
> > > > > >
> > > > > > See this page here (it is used in a way very similar to compare_solutions() and to estimate_solutions(): https://jrosen48.github.io/tidyLPA/reference/bootstrap_lrt.html
> > > > > >
> > > > > > If you have any trouble using it or if you have any questions, please let us know.
> > > > > >
> > > > > > At least based on my initial understanding, the BLRT is recommended over the VLMRT LRT and the adjusted VLMR LRT.
> > > > > >
> > > > > > Regarding the VLMR LRT and adjusted VLMR LRT, these are presently not available. The reason is that tidyLPA is an interface to the mclust software. We have implemented some statistics (SABIC, CAIC, and others) that are not available in mclust; however, based on some initial research, we've had some trouble translating from the publication introducing VLMR LRT by Vuong, Lo, Mendell, and Rubin <https://academic.oup.com/biomet/article-abstract/88/3/767/340113>. Accordingly, the way that we have been able to carry out these tests is using the MPlus software. If you have MPlus, we have written a number of functions that allow you to use it through R/tidyLPA (i.e., estimate_solutions_mplus() <https://jrosen48.github.io/tidyLPA/reference/compare_solutions_mplus.html> and others, see here <https://jrosen48.github.io/tidyLPA/reference/index.html>). However, we have not added functionality to carry out VLMRT LRT and adjusted VLMRT LRT through R/tidyLPA quite yet. If you do have MPlus, we could make these changes quickly. If not and you need to wait for them to be implemented in the other functions - those that do not use MPlus - then that may take awhile (again, because they are not implemented in the mclust package that tidyLPA is an interface to).
> > > > > >
> > > > > > Thank you again for your message - I hope that this helps you to at least be able to carry out the BLRT. The VLMR LRT tests may take a while longer - I hope you understand. Be in touch with any questions and, if you would, to let us know for what purpose you are using the package.
